Section 6 — Detailed Dry Run
Consider a cistern with V = 100 liters and 2 pipes with rates of 10 L/hr and 20 L/hr.

Step	Calculation	Volume Accumulated	Time (hours)
Initial	-	0 liters	0
Simulation	Each hour adds (10 + 20) = 30 L/hr	30 liters	1
60 liters	2
90 liters	3
Partial hour: 100 / 30 ≈ 0.333 hours	100 liters	3.333
Through the optimal method, we calculate:
Net rate = 10 + 20 = 30 L/hr
Time = 100 / 30 ≈ 3.333 hours

Section 7 — Edge Cases
Empty input (no pipes): Should report that filling is impossible.
All pipes have zero or negative rates: The cistern cannot be filled.
Only one pipe: The formula applies directly (T = V / (rate)).
Rates given in different units: Confirm uniformity (e.g., liters per hour).
Extremely large cistern volumes or extremely small rates may affect precision.
Section 8 — Common Mistakes
Failing to check if the net rate is positive, which may lead to division by zero or negative time.
Mixing up units (e.g., mixing minutes with hours), leading to an incorrect computation.
Over-complicating the problem with unnecessary simulation when a direct mathematical formula exists.
Not accounting for the possibility of negative rates (i.e., pipes that are draining the cistern).
Using integer division instead of floating-point arithmetic, resulting in precision loss.
Section 9 — Interview Tips
